\label{sec:layer-sensitivity}

Layer selection is a researcher degree of freedom that can reverse modality comparison conclusions. A composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B, L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Shallow representations are highly compressed (inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers), so a linear probe operates near the noise floor and threshold crossings are noise-driven (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}).

A five-metric ablation reveals that threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) consistently select deep layers: L60 (94\%) for QwQ-32B, L63 (98\%) for R1-32B. Pure balanced accuracy selects layers whose depth varies across data splits and models (7--98\% under 60/20/20; \cref{tab:ba_layer_comparison}). At these layers, all models$^\dagger$ show significant HS advantage with evidence from strong to qualified (Holm-Bonferroni corrected, \cref{sec:evaluation}); at biased layers, only the two 7--8B models survive. While we report five metrics, their information content is not independent: BA, precision, and FPR share a confusion matrix with 2 degrees of freedom (given class marginals); crossing rate and the additive composite are derived from these. The effective number of independent statistical tests is four (one per model), as reflected in our HB correction. The five-metric ablation diagnoses which \emph{aspect} of classification performance drives layer selection bias, not five independent pieces of evidence. Holm-Bonferroni step-down on four model-level tests yields the same result (all pass, with R1-8B FPR $p{=}0.044$ at rank-4 threshold $\alpha/1{=}0.050$). A joint (layer, $W$) grid search over 192 configurations confirms that L60 is near-optimal for QwQ-32B at recommended windows ($W{=}3$--$5$; \cref{app:layer_sweep}). Full per-layer results appear in the same appendix.

\subsection{Baseline Methods}
\label{sec:baselines}

We evaluate seven alternatives to contextualize probe performance (\cref{tab:negative}): BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} and CUSUM~\citep{page1954cusum} for change-point detection; Mahalanobis distance following TrajGuard~\citep{trajguard}; RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation}; two-layer MLP probes; CCS-adapted~\citep{burns2023discovering}; and cross-model transfer via PCA. A position-only baseline ($t/T$) controls for temporal artifacts. Detailed configurations in \cref{app:bocpd_config,app:transfer_details}.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

We report balanced accuracy, precision, pre-CP FPR, coverage, and lead time (steps between first detection and true CP). Step-level metrics evaluate per-step classification; trace-level metrics aggregate across an entire reasoning trace (e.g., trace-level FPR is the fraction of safe traces with any false alarm exceeding $K$ consecutive crossings). Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings; $K$ was set as a design pre-selection before examining performance; sensitivity analysis (\cref{app:k_ablation}) confirms that $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ produces smooth coverage-FPR tradeoffs without qualitative changes in the modality comparison, though a post-hoc rationale for $K{=}5$ is that it corresponds to a sustained commitment signal rather than transient fluctuation. Modality comparisons use two-sided null-centered bootstrap (10{,}000 resamples) with Holm-Bonferroni step-down correction (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models $= 12$ tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$). Within each model, the three metrics are positively correlated by construction, making the correction stricter than under independence. Control task selectivity~\citep{hewitt2019control} validates genuine representational structure (\cref{app:control}). Throughout, $\dagger$ marks conclusions drawn from sample-limited test sets ($n \leq 20$): R1-32B ($n{=}20$) and QwQ-32B ($n{=}19$).

\textit{Mitigation.} SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and Chain-of-Guardrails~\cite{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning} proposes guardrail insertion during reasoning. None of these conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers, while \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, but \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal eleven category-specific refusal directions, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones, consistent with our finding that single-direction methods are insufficient. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance from safe trajectories; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors internal representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026} proposes proactive world models for detection. These methods assume threats from external input manipulation and operate at the token level on instruction-tuned models. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. \citet{kadali2025internal} decompose internal layer representations to separate jailbreak from benign prompts; their results confirm that layer-wise structure carries safety-relevant signal. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Causal abstraction~\cite{geiger2025causal} provides a theoretical foundation for mechanistic interpretability by formalizing when high-level causal models faithfully describe neural computation, grounding the use of internal representations for behavioral prediction. Linear probing has a long history from layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding} through sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing} to comprehensive surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing,rogers2020primer}. Layer-wise analyses reveal hierarchical linguistic structure across depth~\cite{jawahar2019bert,liu2019linguistic}, and information-theoretic formulations address probe complexity~\cite{voita2020information,pimentel2020information}. \citet{hewitt2019control} introduce control tasks to distinguish structure from memorization, which we adopt (\cref{app:control}). \citet{ravfogel2020null} introduce iterative nullspace projection (INLP) for concept removal; \citet{ravfogel2022rlace} extend this to closed-form linear adversarial concept erasure. \citet{belrose2023leace} establish theoretical limits on linear probe extraction; \citet{elazar2021amnesic} test whether probed information is causally used. \citet{tenney2019bert} show syntactic information in middle layers and semantics in deeper layers. \citet{marks2024geometry} find emergent linear structure in LLM truth representations, consistent with the linear separability we observe for safety commitment. \citet{zhang2025probing_selfverify} apply hidden-state probing to self-verification in reasoning models, a complementary perspective on what reasoning-model representations encode. Concurrent work by \citet{alvarez2026transport_geometry} uses hidden-state geometry for step-level hallucination detection; the approach shares our temporal probing framework but targets a different phenomenon. \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show that linear probes can rely on textual evidence rather than deeper representations, which motivates our comparison of hidden-state probes against text-based monitors. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis. We demonstrate that this practice can reverse a study's central conclusion (\cref{sec:layer-results}). Our probing approach establishes correlational evidence; causal validation via distributed alignment search~\citep{geiger2025causal}, LEACE~\citep{belrose2023leace}, or inference-time intervention~\citep{li2024iti} would be needed to confirm that probed directions are causally active in safety reasoning. We focus on establishing the observational foundation and identifying the layer selection confound before pursuing causal follow-ups.

\paragraph{Change-Point Detection.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B). These failures motivate supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.

